---
const requirements = [
  { item: 'Python 3.13+', detail: 'plus uv for dependency management' },
  { item: 'PostgreSQL 15+', detail: 'local or remote; the source of truth' },
  { item: 'Valkey / Redis', detail: 'sessions and cache' },
  { item: 'wireguard-tools', detail: 'the wg CLI' },
  { item: 'iproute2', detail: 'the ip CLI — creates and addresses wg0' },
  { item: 'nftables', detail: 'the nft CLI — per-user chains and NAT' },
  { item: 'CAP_NET_ADMIN', detail: 'required to manage the interface and firewall' },
  { item: 'WireGuard kernel module', detail: 'in-tree since Linux 5.6' },
];

const troubleshooting = [
  {
    symptom: 'RuntimeError: Operation not permitted on ip link add',
    cause: 'The service has no CAP_NET_ADMIN.',
    fix: 'Check AmbientCapabilities in the unit; in an unprivileged LXC, confirm the host loaded the wireguard module.',
  },
  {
    symptom: 'nftables is not available in Admin → Diagnostics',
    cause: 'nft cannot talk to the kernel, usually a missing capability or nf_tables module.',
    fix: 'Run nft list ruleset as the service user; modprobe nf_tables nft_masq on the host if it fails.',
  },
  {
    symptom: 'Peers connect but reach nothing',
    cause: 'IP forwarding is off, so packets die at the server.',
    fix: 'Verify sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward returns 1 inside the container or VM, not just on the host.',
  },
  {
    symptom: 'Clients get a config but never handshake',
    cause: 'WG_WG_ENDPOINT_HOST or the UDP port is wrong or blocked.',
    fix: 'The endpoint must be the address clients can reach from outside, and the UDP port must be forwarded to this host.',
  },
  {
    symptom: 'Addresses vanish from wg0',
    cause: 'A host network manager claimed the interface.',
    fix: 'Exclude wg0 from systemd-networkd, NetworkManager or dhcpcd.',
  },
];
---

<section id="bare-metal" class="py-20 sm:py-28">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-950 sm:text-4xl">
        Bare metal: VM or LXC
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600">
        Docker is the recommended path, but nothing in WireGUI requires it. Here is the full
        install on a plain VM or an LXC container.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- What you are signing up for -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-5xl">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
          WireGUI is a Python application that shells out to <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">ip</code>,
          <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">wg</code> and
          <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">nft</code>.
          The container image adds no magic &mdash; it installs those three tools, runs
          <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">alembic upgrade head</code>, and starts
          <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">python -m wiregui.main</code> with
          <code class="rounded bg-white px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">NET_ADMIN</code>.
          A bare-metal install does exactly the same thing under systemd. What you give up is the
          bundled PostgreSQL, Valkey and VictoriaMetrics &mdash; you provide those yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Requirements -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">Requirements</h3>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {requirements.map(({ item, detail }) => (
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4">
            <svg class="mt-0.5 h-5 w-5 flex-none text-navy-700" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4.5 12.75l6 6 9-13.5" />
            </svg>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold text-navy-950">{item}</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">{detail}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        System packages
      </h3>

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Debian / Ubuntu</h4>
          <div class="mt-3 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
            <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>apt update
apt install -y python3 git curl \
    wireguard-tools iproute2 nftables \
    postgresql valkey-server

<span class="text-slate-500"># uv, installed system-wide</span>
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh \
    | env UV_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            On releases without a <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">valkey-server</code> package, use
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">redis-server</code> &mdash; the wire protocol is the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Fedora / RHEL / Rocky</h4>
          <div class="mt-3 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
            <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>dnf install -y python3 git curl \
    wireguard-tools iproute nftables \
    postgresql-server valkey

postgresql-setup --initdb
systemctl enable --now postgresql valkey

curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh \
    | env UV_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
          <strong class="text-navy-950">Python version.</strong> WireGUI needs 3.13+. If your distro ships something older,
          you do not need a PPA &mdash; <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">uv</code> will fetch
          the right interpreter itself with <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">uv python install 3.13</code>,
          and <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">uv sync</code> will use it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        Database and cache
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Create the role and database WireGUI will own. Valkey needs no configuration for a
        single-host install; it just has to be listening.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>sudo -u postgres psql -c "CREATE USER wiregui WITH PASSWORD 'choose-a-real-password';"
sudo -u postgres psql -c "CREATE DATABASE wiregui OWNER wiregui;"

systemctl enable --now postgresql valkey-server</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
        Install WireGUI
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        A dedicated system user owns the code and runs the service. It needs no login shell and no
        home directory beyond the install path.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>useradd --system --home /opt/wiregui --shell /usr/sbin/nologin wiregui

git clone https://github.com/bartei/wiregui.git /opt/wiregui
chown -R wiregui:wiregui /opt/wiregui

<span class="text-slate-500"># Build the virtualenv in /opt/wiregui/.venv</span>
cd /opt/wiregui
sudo -u wiregui uv sync --no-dev</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Dependencies install from prebuilt wheels on glibc x86-64 and arm64. On musl distributions
        such as Alpine, <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">lxml</code> and
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">xmlsec</code> (pulled in by SAML support) compile from source
        &mdash; add <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">build-base libxml2-dev libxslt-dev xmlsec-dev pkgconfig</code> first.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
        Configuration
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Every setting is an environment variable with the <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm font-medium text-navy-700">WG_</code> prefix.
        Put them in a file systemd reads, not in the shell history.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-white/10 px-4 py-2">
          <span class="ml-1 text-xs text-slate-400">/etc/wiregui/wiregui.env</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>WG_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://wiregui:choose-a-real-password@localhost/wiregui
WG_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

<span class="text-slate-500"># openssl rand -hex 32 — rotating this invalidates sessions and</span>
<span class="text-slate-500"># the encrypted secrets already stored in the database</span>
WG_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-32-random-bytes

<span class="text-slate-500"># WireGuard</span>
WG_WG_ENABLED=true
WG_WG_ENDPOINT_HOST=vpn.example.com
WG_WG_ENDPOINT_PORT=51820
WG_WG_IPV4_NETWORK=10.3.2.0/24
WG_WG_IPV6_NETWORK=fd00::3:2:0/120

<span class="text-slate-500"># Web</span>
WG_HOST=0.0.0.0
WG_PORT=13000
WG_EXTERNAL_URL=https://vpn.example.com
WG_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com

<span class="text-slate-500"># systemd captures stderr into the journal already</span>
WG_LOG_TO_FILE=false</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
            <strong class="text-navy-950">Lock the file down.</strong> It holds the database password and the key that
            encrypts stored secrets: <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">chmod 640</code>,
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">chown root:wiregui</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
            <strong class="text-navy-950">Leave <code>WG_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> unset.</strong> On first run WireGUI generates one
            and writes it to the log. Set it only if you are provisioning automatically.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
        The full variable list is in the <a href="#documentation" class="font-medium text-navy-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-navy-900">configuration reference</a> below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
        Kernel forwarding
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        A VPN server routes between the tunnel and everything else, so forwarding has to be on.
        This is per network namespace: setting it on an LXC host does <em>not</em> set it inside the container.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code><span class="text-slate-500"># /etc/sysctl.d/99-wiregui.conf</span>
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding = 1

<span class="text-slate-500"># Apply now</span>
sysctl --system</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 6 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
        Migrations and first run
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        Alembic reads <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm font-medium text-navy-700">WG_DATABASE_URL</code> from the
        same environment, so export the file before running it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>cd /opt/wiregui
set -a; . /etc/wiregui/wiregui.env; set +a

sudo -u wiregui --preserve-env uv run alembic upgrade head</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Run this once before the first start, and again after every upgrade. The service itself does
        not migrate &mdash; only the Docker image does that on boot.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 7 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">7</span>
        systemd unit
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        WireGUI needs <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm font-medium text-navy-700">CAP_NET_ADMIN</code> and nothing else
        privileged. Granting it as an ambient capability keeps the process off root.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-white/10 px-4 py-2">
          <span class="ml-1 text-xs text-slate-400">/etc/systemd/system/wiregui.service</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>[Unit]
Description=WireGUI
After=network-online.target postgresql.service valkey-server.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=wiregui
Group=wiregui
WorkingDirectory=/opt/wiregui
EnvironmentFile=/etc/wiregui/wiregui.env
ExecStart=/opt/wiregui/.venv/bin/python -m wiregui.main
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5

<span class="text-slate-500"># Manage wg0 and the nftables ruleset without being root</span>
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_ADMIN
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_ADMIN
NoNewPrivileges=yes

<span class="text-slate-500"># Hardening that does not interfere with networking.</span>
<span class="text-slate-500"># NiceGUI keeps session storage in .nicegui/ under the working</span>
<span class="text-slate-500"># directory, so that tree has to stay writable.</span>
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
ReadWritePaths=/opt/wiregui

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable --now wiregui

<span class="text-slate-500"># The generated admin password appears here on first start</span>
journalctl -u wiregui -f</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-amber-900">
          <strong>Do not put wg0 under a host network manager.</strong> systemd-networkd, NetworkManager and dhcpcd
          will happily claim an interface they did not create and strip its addresses. WireGUI restores
          them periodically and logs a warning when it has to, but the fix is to exclude
          <code class="rounded bg-white/60 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium">wg0</code> from the manager.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 8 -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">
        <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 flex-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-navy-950 text-sm font-bold text-white" aria-hidden="true">8</span>
        Verify
      </h3>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code><span class="text-slate-500"># The interface exists and carries the tunnel addresses</span>
ip addr show wg0

<span class="text-slate-500"># The server key and listen port are set</span>
wg show wg0

<span class="text-slate-500"># WireGUI's own nftables table is present</span>
nft list table inet wiregui

<span class="text-slate-500"># The app answers</span>
curl -s localhost:13000/api/health</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        All four should succeed. <em>Admin &rarr; Diagnostics</em> shows the same state from the UI, including the
        live ruleset. Put a reverse proxy in front for TLS &mdash; see
        <a href="#documentation" class="font-medium text-navy-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-navy-900">Deployment</a> for nginx and Caddy configs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- LXC -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">LXC containers</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        An LXC container shares the host kernel, so WireGuard works from inside it as long as the host
        provides the module and the container is allowed to run its own network namespace. This is the
        normal case on Proxmox: an <strong>unprivileged</strong> container is enough &mdash; it holds
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">CAP_NET_ADMIN</code> within its own
        namespace, which is all WireGUI touches.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-navy-950">On the host, once</h4>
          <div class="mt-3 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
            <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code><span class="text-slate-500"># Containers cannot load modules — the host must</span>
modprobe wireguard
modprobe nf_tables
modprobe nft_masq

<span class="text-slate-500"># Make it survive a reboot</span>
printf 'wireguard\nnf_tables\nnft_masq\n' \
    &gt; /etc/modules-load.d/wiregui.conf</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Inside the container, check</h4>
          <div class="mt-3 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
            <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code><span class="text-slate-500"># Can we create a WireGuard interface at all?</span>
ip link add wgtest type wireguard \
    &amp;&amp; ip link del wgtest &amp;&amp; echo OK

<span class="text-slate-500"># Can we program nftables?</span>
nft list ruleset &gt;/dev/null &amp;&amp; echo OK

<span class="text-slate-500"># Is forwarding on in THIS namespace?</span>
sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Run these before installing anything. If the first one fails, the host is missing the module
            or the container profile blocks it, and no amount of WireGUI configuration will help.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
        <h4 class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Proxmox notes</h4>
        <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span class="text-navy-700" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              Kernel WireGuard needs no <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">/dev/net/tun</code> passthrough.
              Guides that add it are configuring the userspace <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">wireguard-go</code>
              implementation, which WireGUI does not use.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span class="text-navy-700" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              If the container also runs PostgreSQL and Valkey, give it at least 2&nbsp;GB of RAM. WireGUI
              itself is small; PostgreSQL is what wants headroom.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span class="text-navy-700" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              Forward the WireGuard UDP port from the host or router to the container's address, and set
              <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-navy-800">WG_WG_ENDPOINT_HOST</code> to whatever clients
              see from the internet &mdash; not the container's private IP.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span class="text-navy-700" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
            <span>
              A privileged container works too, but it buys nothing here and costs isolation.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- VM -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">Virtual machines</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        A VM has its own kernel, so there is no host to coordinate with: steps 1&ndash;8 above are the whole
        install. Two things still bite people.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <p class="font-semibold text-navy-950">The hypervisor may filter</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Routing traffic for the tunnel subnet means emitting packets with source addresses that are not
            the VM's own. Anti-spoofing or MAC filtering on the virtual NIC will silently drop them &mdash; on
            VMware disable MAC address changes and forged transmits, on Proxmox leave the firewall's
            IP filter off for that interface.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <p class="font-semibold text-navy-950">MTU</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            WireGUI hands clients an MTU of 1280, which is conservative enough for nested tunnels and most
            overlay networks. If your path is clean you can raise it, but 1280 is the safe default and
            rarely worth changing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Upgrades -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">Upgrading</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
        The database is the source of truth and the interface is rebuilt from it on every start, so an
        upgrade is a pull, a sync, a migration and a restart.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-navy-950">
        <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>cd /opt/wiregui
sudo -u postgres pg_dump wiregui &gt; /var/backups/wiregui-$(date +%F).sql

systemctl stop wiregui
sudo -u wiregui git pull
sudo -u wiregui uv sync --no-dev

set -a; . /etc/wiregui/wiregui.env; set +a
sudo -u wiregui --preserve-env uv run alembic upgrade head

systemctl start wiregui</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Peers, rules and keys all live in PostgreSQL. That dump is the backup &mdash; there is no separate
        WireGuard state to preserve.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Troubleshooting -->
    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-navy-950">Troubleshooting</h3>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50">
              <tr>
                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-navy-950">Symptom</th>
                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-navy-950">Cause</th>
                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-navy-950">Fix</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 bg-white">
              {troubleshooting.map(({ symptom, cause, fix }) => (
                <tr class="hover:bg-slate-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top"><code class="text-xs text-navy-800">{symptom}</code></td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-slate-600">{cause}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-slate-600">{fix}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Still stuck? Open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/bartei/wiregui/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="font-medium text-navy-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-navy-900">GitHub</a>
        with the output of the four verification commands above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
